WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.480 --> 00:00:05.760
International forces in Mali have been trying&nbsp;
to help fight Islamist groups which in 2012&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:11.120
threatened to take over the whole country.&nbsp;
Nearly ten years and over 10 billion euros&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:16.320
of international funding later, huge swathes of&nbsp;
the country are still out of government control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:23.120
The UN has peacekeepers there, and EU troops&nbsp;
also train local soldiers. DW's West Africa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:29.840
correspondent Fred Muvunyi went to Koulikoro –&nbsp;
just outside the capital Bamako for this report.&nbsp;

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:41.040
Malian soldiers learn about Improvised Explosive&nbsp;
Devices - how to find them, how to defuse them.&nbsp;

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:48.880
IEDs are a plague in places like&nbsp;
Mali – effectively home-made mines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:55.280
which kill soldiers and civilians alike.
Germany is leading the training mission&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.320
on behalf of the EU. The trainers&nbsp;
are confident things are improving.&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:09.280
The Europeans may be able to give Malians western&nbsp;
technical know-how. But Malian forces still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:16.080
operate firmly in an autocratic environment.
We wanted to hear from a Malian soldier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:22.000
but a local commander told us this was&nbsp;
absolutely forbidden. We understood that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:27.200
a soldier could be physically harmed if they&nbsp;
were to break ranks and talk to the media.&nbsp;

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:35.600
"International forces like these have been here&nbsp;
in Mali for years - trying to help the government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:42.880
fight jihadist groups. Foreign governments have&nbsp;
poured money as well as sending soldiers to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.880 --> 00:01:49.120
try to build solid local security forces.&nbsp;
But the jihadists are not pulling back."&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:57.760
The violence has forced many Malians to leave&nbsp;
their villages and seek refuge here in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:05.840
capital, Bamako. It’s created a mix of people,&nbsp;
all hoping *someone* will stop the fighting.&nbsp;

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:14.320
"The international community should let&nbsp;
the transition government do its job."&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:24.960
"We need the international to extend its&nbsp;
support. So that we get out of this crisis."&nbsp;

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:29.600
"To take Mali out of this problem,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:35.840
poor economy, and insecurity, we&nbsp;
need France to leave this country"&nbsp;

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:44.640
The EU mission still has three years of its&nbsp;
mandate to run. They still have much to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:51.280
to enable Malian forces to defend the government&nbsp;
against the Islamists - from the air as well as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:57.760
on the ground. These soldiers are learning&nbsp;
to call in air strikes accurately.&nbsp;

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:04.800
"They need to study a lot of topography because&nbsp;
they need to be precise when they go on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:12.800
ground. We are talking about an aircraft attacking&nbsp;
on the ground, so we need to be precise about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:20.320
we are saying and the coordinates we brought on&nbsp;
the map. We need to avoid the collateral damages."&nbsp;

00:03:23.120 --> 00:03:29.200
The Malian air force is still very small –&nbsp;
a few attack planes, some helicopters and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.200 --> 00:03:38.160
one cargo plane. They’re training for a future&nbsp;
when they’ll be taking on their enemy alone.

